{%- comment -%}
  Request a quote. Brief §9.6, and the decision behind its shape was taken
  explicitly: B2B does not use the ecommerce checkout, it goes straight to
  WhatsApp (brief §16, "-> langsung ke WA").

  So this is NOT a form that posts anywhere. It is three fields and a link: the
  fields are composed into a WhatsApp message and the submit hands the reader to
  wa.me with it already typed. Nothing is stored, nothing is emailed, and there
  is no inbox to check — the conversation starts in the place the studio already
  answers enquiries, which is what the reviews describe them being good at.

  WHY NOT SHOPIFY'S OWN CONTACT FORM. It would give a record, and it was
  considered and rejected for this section: an enquiry that lands in an email
  nobody has set up to watch is worse than no form, and the studio's answer to
  every other enquiry channel is WhatsApp. The contact page carries the native
  form for the general case; the quote route is deliberately one channel.

  HOW IT WORKS WITHOUT JAVASCRIPT — and it does, which is the whole reason it is
  shaped this way rather than as a click handler on a button.

  The element is a real `<form method="get">` whose action is the wa.me URL. The
  three visible fields have NO `name` attribute, so they are never serialised;
  the only named control is the hidden `text` input the script fills in. With
  script off, submitting sends `?text=` empty and WhatsApp opens the chat with
  the studio and nothing typed — the reader is exactly where they were trying to
  get to, and types their question themselves. With script on they arrive with
  their answers already in the message box. Enhancement, not a requirement.

  `required` still works in the no-script case: native validation does not care
  whether a field has a name.

  THE FIELD LABELS ARE PASSED TO THE SCRIPT AS DATA ATTRIBUTES, never hardcoded
  in theme.js. `data-quote-field` on each input carries the label the message
  should use, exactly as save-button.liquid passes its two states. That keeps
  every user-visible string in the locale file where it can be translated, and it
  is why the composed message reads "Name: ..." in the site's language rather
  than in whatever language somebody typed into a JavaScript file.

  NO FILE UPLOAD, and the brief asked for one ("Upload Design / Reference jika
  memungkinkan"). It is not possible down this route: a wa.me deep link carries
  text and nothing else, and Shopify's own contact form tag accepts no file input
  either. Rather than drop the requirement silently, the note under the
  fields says where to send a reference — which is the next message in a chat
  that is already open. If a real upload is wanted, it needs the Shopify Forms
  app and this section is replaced, not extended.

  WITH NO WHATSAPP NUMBER SET the form still renders at full size and the submit
  is unavailable rather than absent, with an alert saying why. That is the
  placeholder rule applied to a control: the layout is final before the setting
  is filled in, and a disabled button that explains itself beats a button that
  opens a broken wa.me URL. The number is a theme setting and is blank today.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign wa_number = settings.whatsapp_number | strip
  # wa.me wants digits only — no +, no spaces, no dashes. Doing it here rather
  # than asking the studio to remember the format is one less way for the one
  # link the B2B page depends on to be silently wrong.
  assign wa_digits = wa_number | remove: '+' | remove: ' ' | remove: '-' | remove: '(' | remove: ')'
  assign wa_ready = false
  if wa_digits != blank
    assign wa_ready = true
  endif
  assign wa_missing = true
  if wa_ready
    assign wa_missing = false
  endif
-%}

<section
  id="quote"
  aria-labelledby="quote-heading"
  class="relative isolate scroll-mt-(--scroll-offset) overflow-hidden border-y border-rule bg-canvas"
>
  {% render 'aurora', tone: 'canvas', origin: 'bottom-left' %}

  <div class="relative z-10 mx-auto grid w-full max-w-content gap-block px-gutter py-section lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-block-lg">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 id="quote-heading" class="text-h1 leading-h1">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.heading, label: 'heading' %}
      </h2>

      <p class="wl-measure text-body leading-body text-ink-muted">
        {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.body, label: 'body', lines: 3 %}
      </p>
    </div>

    <form
      method="get"
      action="https://wa.me/{{ wa_digits }}"
      data-quote-form
      class="flex flex-col gap-4.5"
    >
      <input type="hidden" name="text" data-quote-text value="">

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <label for="quote-name" class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">{{ 'quote.name' | t }}</label>
        <input
          id="quote-name"
          type="text"
          required
          autocomplete="name"
          data-quote-field="{{ 'quote.name' | t }}"
          class="min-h-13 rounded-sm border border-line bg-surface px-4 text-body text-ink placeholder:text-ink-subtle"
        >
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <label for="quote-whatsapp" class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">{{ 'quote.whatsapp' | t }}</label>
        {%- comment -%}
          `type="tel"` rather than `number`: a phone number is a string of digits
          with a leading zero or a country code, and a number input strips the
          zero, offers a spinner and rejects the `+`. `inputmode="tel"` is what
          actually brings up the phone keypad.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input
          id="quote-whatsapp"
          type="tel"
          inputmode="tel"
          required
          autocomplete="tel"
          data-quote-field="{{ 'quote.whatsapp' | t }}"
          class="min-h-13 rounded-sm border border-line bg-surface px-4 text-body text-ink placeholder:text-ink-subtle"
        >
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <label for="quote-description" class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">{{ 'quote.description' | t }}</label>
        <textarea
          id="quote-description"
          rows="5"
          required
          data-quote-field="{{ 'quote.description' | t }}"
          class="rounded-sm border border-line bg-surface px-4 py-3 text-body leading-body text-ink placeholder:text-ink-subtle"
        ></textarea>
      </div>

      {%- if section.settings.note != blank -%}
        <p class="text-caption leading-snug text-ink-muted">{{ section.settings.note }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if wa_missing -%}
        {%- assign missing_body = 'quote.no_number' | t -%}
        {% render 'alert', tone: 'info', body: missing_body %}
      {%- endif -%}

      {% render 'button',
        label: section.settings.cta,
        variant: 'primary',
        type: 'submit',
        disabled: wa_missing,
        class: 'w-full px-6 sm:px-11' %}
    </form>
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Request a quote",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The submit hands the reader to WhatsApp with their answers already typed. Set the number under Theme settings → Custom & business; until it is set the button is unavailable and says so. There is no inbox behind this form — the conversation happens in WhatsApp." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "Request a quote" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Body", "default": "Tell us what your organisation needs and we will come back with a price against the specification and the quantity." },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "note", "label": "Note under the fields", "default": "Have a design or a reference? Send it in the chat once WhatsApp opens — the form itself cannot carry a file." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta", "label": "Button", "default": "Request a quote" }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Request a quote" }]
}
{% endschema %}
